{"type":"message","id":"7ea36e2a","parentId":"f2eb8e5f","timestamp":"2026-03-08T22:14:35.408Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sun 2026-03-08 23:13 GMT+1] You are an expert AI systems architect.\n\n**Context:**\n- OpenClaw has Python neuro-memory-agent with 8 brain mechanisms\n- Evolution service needs to query neuro-memory for failure patterns\n- Need to decide: query interface design\n\n**Question:** What's the best query interface between evolution (TypeScript) and neuro-memory (Python)?\n\nOptions:\n1. **Direct MCP call**: evolution → neuroMemoryBridge.retrieve(query)\n2. **Python script spawn**: evolution spawns python analyze.py --for-evolution, reads JSON stdout\n3. **Shared SQLite**: both read/write to same database\n4. **REST API**: Python exposes HTTP endpoint, evolution calls it\n\nAnalyze:\n- Latency (evolution needs response in seconds, not minutes)\n- Data richness (what can be returned)\n- Error handling\n- Implementation complexity\n\nGive me:\n- Recommendation with confidence %\n- Why this beats alternatives\n- Interface contract (what queries, what responses)\n- Error scenarios and fallbacks\n\nBe specific and opinionated."}],"timestamp":1773008075407}}
{"type":"message","id":"d6b2837b","parentId":"7ea36e2a","timestamp":"2026-03-08T22:16:04.017Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to analyze the best query interface between evolution (TypeScript) and neuro-memory (Python) for OpenClaw. Let me break down each option systematically.\n\nKey requirements:\n- Evolution needs response in seconds (not minutes) - so latency is critical\n- Need to query failure patterns from neuro-memory\n- 8 brain mechanisms in neuro-memory agent\n- TypeScript evolution service, Python neuro-memory service\n\nLet me analyze each option:\n\n**Option 1: Direct MCP call** (evolution → neuroMemoryBridge.retrieve(query))\n- Pros:\n  - Already have MCP infrastructure in OpenClaw\n  - Type-safe if well-defined\n  - Direct method call, low overhead\n  - Can leverage existing neuroMemoryBridge\n  - Good error propagation\n- Cons:\n  - MCP may have overhead\n  - Need to ensure neuro-memory is accessible via MCP\n  - Might require neuro-memory to be running as MCP server\n- Latency: Low-Medium (depends on MCP implementation)\n- Complexity: Low-Medium\n- Data richness: High (can return complex objects)\n\n**Option 2: Python script spawn** (evolution spawns python analyze.py --for-evolution, reads JSON stdout)\n- Pros:\n  - Simple, no server needed\n  - Can spawn on-demand\n  - Easy to debug\n  - No dependency on running service\n- Cons:\n  - Process spawn overhead (100-500ms just to start Python)\n  - Memory overhead (loading Python interpreter + neuro-memory each time)\n  - No caching/warm state\n  - Harder to handle errors\n  - JSON serialization limits\n- Latency: Medium-High (process spawn + initialization + query)\n- Complexity: Low\n- Data richness: Medium (JSON limits)\n\n**Option 3: Shared SQLite** (both read/write to same database)\n- Pros:\n  - No inter-process communication needed\n  - Very fast reads\n  - Simple implementation\n- Cons:\n  - Locking issues (SQLite has limited concurrent access)\n  - No logic - just raw data access\n  - Can't leverage neuro-memory's brain mechanisms (they're code, not data)\n  - Need to understand neuro-memory's schema\n  - Schema changes break evolution\n  - Coupling is very tight\n- Latency: Very Low\n- Complexity: Medium (need to understand schema)\n- Data richness: Low (raw data, no processed insights)\n\n**Option 4: REST API** (Python exposes HTTP endpoint, evolution calls it)\n- Pros:\n  - Standard, well-understood\n  - Can return complex JSON\n  - Easy to test/debug\n  - Can use HTTP caching\n  - Good error handling (HTTP status codes)\n  - Neuro-memory stays running (warm state)\n- Cons:\n  - HTTP overhead\n  - Need to manage Python service lifecycle\n  - Port management\n- Latency: Low (HTTP call, warm service)\n- Complexity: Medium\n- Data richness: High (structured JSON)\n\n**Analysis for Evolution Use Case:**\n\nEvolution needs:\n- Fast response (seconds, not minutes)\n- Failure patterns (likely need processed insights, not raw data)\n- Reliability (error handling critical)\n- Simplicity (evolution is already complex)\n\n**Comparison:**\n\n1. **MCP call** - If neuro-memory already has MCP integration, this is ideal\n\n. Clean, type-safe, leverages existing infrastructure.\n\n2. **Python spawn** - Too slow for evolution's needs. Every spawn is 200-500ms + Python initialization. Unacceptable latency.\n\n3. **Shared SQLite** - Can't leverage brain mechanisms! The value of neuro-memory is in its 8 brain mechanisms that process and analyze memory. SQLite gives raw data access but loses the intelligence layer. Also tight coupling.\n\n4. **REST API** - Solid option if neuro-memory runs as a service. Lower overhead than spawn, but still need to manage service lifecycle.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\nIf neuro-memory already has MCP bridge (neuroMemoryBridge exists), go with **Option 1: Direct MCP call**.\n\nIf no MCP bridge exists and we need to build from scratch, go with **Option 4: REST API** with neuro-memory running as a lightweight FastAPI service.\n\n**Why MCP wins (if available):**\n- Already integrated in OpenClaw ecosystem\n- Lower overhead than HTTP\n- Type-safe contracts\n- Evolution can use existing neuroMemoryBridge\n- No additional service management\n\n**Why REST API beats others (if building fresh):**\n- Faster than process spawn (warm service)\n- More decoupled than SQLite (can use brain mechanisms)\n- Standard, easy to debug\n- Can return processed insights, not just raw data\n- Evolution just makes HTTP calls\n\n**Why SQLite fails for this use case:**\n- Can't access brain mechanisms (they're code!)\n- Tight coupling to schema\n- Evolution would need to reimplement analysis logic\n\n**Why process spawn fails:**\n- Latency unacceptable (200-500ms per spawn)\n- No warm state/caching\n- Resource inefficient\n\nLet me design the interface contract for the recommended approach...","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"# Query Interface Analysis: Evolution ↔ Neuro-Memory\n\n## Recommendation: **Option 1 - Direct MCP Call** with **90% confidence**\n\n*If MCP bridge doesn't exist: Option 4 (REST API) with 75% confidence*\n\n---\n\n## Why MCP Call Beats Alternatives\n\n### **Latency Comparison** (estimated)\n| Option | Latency | Why |\n|--------|---------|-----|\n| **MCP call** | **10-50ms** | In-process call to bridge, shared memory with Python worker |\n| REST API | 20-100ms | HTTP overhead, serialization, network stack |\n| Python spawn | 200-800ms | Process spawn + Python interpreter + module loading |\n| Shared SQLite | 1-10ms | Direct file read, **BUT** loses brain mechanisms |\n\n**Winner: MCP** - Fast enough, but more importantly: **preserves intelligence**\n\n### **Critical Insight: The Brain Mechanisms Problem**\n\nOptions 3 (SQLite) fails because neuro-memory's value isn't the data—it's the **8 brain mechanisms** that process it:\n- Pattern recognition\n- Failure clustering\n- Temporal correlation\n- Contextual retrieval\n\n**SQLite gives raw memories, not analyzed patterns.** Evolution would need to reimplement analysis in TypeScript = duplication nightmare.\n\n---\n\n## Why Not Other Options\n\n### ❌ **Python Script Spawn**\n- **Latency killer**: 200-800ms per query is unacceptable for evolution cycles\n- **Cold start every time**: Python loads modules, initializes brain mechanisms\n- **Resource waste**: Spawning processes for every query\n- **Use case**: Only for one-off batch analysis, not real-time queries\n\n### ❌ **Shared SQLite**\n- **Loses intelligence**: Can't call brain mechanisms through a database\n- **Tight coupling**: Schema changes break evolution\n- **Locking hell**: SQLite + concurrent access = pain\n- **Use case**: Good for bulk data export/migration, not querying\n\n### ⚠️ **REST API** (backup option)\n- **Pros**: Standard, debuggable, language-agnostic\n- **Cons**: Need to manage Python service lifecycle, HTTP overhead\n- **Use case**: Best if building from scratch without MCP infrastructure\n\n---\n\n## Interface Contract\n\n### **Query Types**\n\n```typescript\n// MCP method: neuroMemoryBridge.queryForEvolution\n\ninterface EvolutionQuery {\n  type: 'failure_patterns' | 'similar_failures' | 'temporal_trends' | 'context_search';\n  \n  // For failure_patterns\n  timeRange?: {\n    from: Date;\n    to: Date;\n  };\n  \n  // For similar_failures\n  failureSignature?: {\n    errorType: string;\n    component?: string;\n    stackTraceHash?: string;\n  };\n  \n  // For context_search\n  context?: {\n    proposalId?: string;\n    fileType?: string;\n    operation?: string;\n  };\n  \n  // Common options\n  limit?: number;\n  minConfidence?: number; // 0-1, filter low-confidence patterns\n}\n```\n\n### **Response Types**\n\n```typescript\ninterface EvolutionQueryResponse {\n  success: boolean;\n  \n  // When success=true\n  patterns?: FailurePattern[];\n  similarEvents?: MemoryEvent[];\n  trends?: TrendAnalysis;\n  \n  // When success=false\n  error?: {\n    code: 'TIMEOUT' | 'NO_DATA' | 'INVALID_QUERY' | 'BRAIN_ERROR';\n    message: string;\n    retryable: boolean;\n  };\n  \n  metadata: {\n    queryTime: number; // ms\n    brainMechanismsUsed: string[];\n    confidence: number; // 0-1, overall confidence in results\n  };\n}\n\ninterface FailurePattern {\n  id: string;\n  pattern_type: 'recurrent' | 'cascading' | 'periodic' | 'contextual';\n  \n  // Core pattern data\n  occurrences: number;\n  firstSeen: Date;\n  lastSeen: Date;\n  \n  // Analysis from brain mechanisms\n  correlations: string[]; // e.g., [\"high_load\", \"weekend\", \"first_proposal_of_day\"]\n  suggestedActions: string[];\n  \n  // For evolution specifically\n  relevantToEvolution: boolean;\n  confidence: number;\n}\n```\n\n### **Example Queries**\n\n```typescript\n// Query 1: Get recent failure patterns\nconst query: EvolutionQuery = {\n  type: 'failure_patterns',\n  timeRange: {\n    from: new Date(Date.now() - 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000), // last 7 days\n    to: new Date()\n  },\n  minConfidence: 0.7,\n  limit: 10\n};\n\n// Query 2: Find similar failures to current proposal failure\nconst query: EvolutionQuery = {\n  type: 'similar_failures',\n  failureSignature: {\n    errorType: 'TEST_FAILURE',\n    component: 'evolution/generator.ts',\n    stackTraceHash: 'abc123'\n  },\n  limit: 5\n};\n\n// Query 3: Temporal trends for evolution proposals\nconst query: EvolutionQuery = {\n  type: 'temporal_trends',\n  context: {\n    fileType: 'typescript',\n    operation: 'evolution_proposal'\n  },\n  timeRange: {\n    from: new Date(Date.now() - 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000), // last 30 days\n    to: new Date()\n  }\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n## Error Scenarios & Fallbacks\n\n### **1. Neuro-Memory Unavailable**\n```typescript\n// Detection\nresponse.error.code === 'BRAIN_ERROR'\n\n// Fallback Strategy\nif (!response.success && response.error?.retryable) {\n  // Fallback 1: Retry with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s)\n  await retry(query, { maxAttempts: 3, backoff: 'exponential' });\n  \n  // Fallback 2: Use cached patterns from last successful query\n  const cachedPatterns = await evolutionCache.get('last_known_patterns');\n  \n  // Fallback 3: Proceed without neuro-memory (evolution still works)\n  logger.warn('Proceeding without neuro-memory insights');\n  return []; // Evolution continues with degraded intelligence\n}\n```\n\n### **2. Query Timeout (>2s)**\n```typescript\n// Timeout threshold: 2000ms\nconst response = await neuroMemoryBridge.queryForEvolution(query, {\n  timeout: 2000\n});\n\nif (timedOut) {\n  // Fallback: Use simpler query\n  const simplifiedQuery = {\n    type: 'context_search',\n    limit: 3 // Smaller result set\n  };\n  \n  // Or skip and continue\n  logger.warn('Neuro-memory query timed out, skipping');\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\n### **3. No Relevant Patterns Found**\n```typescript\nif (response.success && response.patterns.length === 0) {\n  // This is OK! Evolution can proceed\n  logger.info('No failure patterns found in neuro-memory');\n  \n  // Maybe too recent? Try broader time range\n  if (query.timeRange) {\n    const broaderQuery = {\n      ...query,\n      timeRange: {\n        from: new Date(query.timeRange.from.getTime() - 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000),\n        to: query.timeRange.to\n      }\n    };\n    return await query(broaderQuery);\n  }\n  \n  return [];\n}\n```\n\n### **4. Invalid Query / Schema Mismatch**\n```typescript\nif (response.error?.code === 'INVALID_QUERY') {\n  // Log and alert\n  logger.error('Invalid neuro-memory query', { query, error: response.error });\n  \n  // Fallback: Use default query\n  return await neuroMemoryBridge.queryForEvolution({\n    type: 'failure_patterns',\n    timeRange: { from: last7Days, to: now },\n    limit: 5\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### **5. Low Confidence Results (<0.5)**\n```typescript\nif (response.metadata.confidence < 0.5) {\n  logger.warn('Low confidence neuro-memory results', {\n    confidence: response.metadata.confidence,\n    patterns: response.patterns.length\n  });\n  \n  // Decision: Use results but flag them\n  return response.patterns.map(p => ({\n    ...p,\n    lowConfidence: true // Evolution can use but be cautious\n  }));\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Complexity\n\n| Aspect | MCP Call | REST API | Python Spawn | SQLite |\n|--------|----------|----------|--------------|--------|\n| **Setup** | Low (if bridge exists) | Medium (service mgmt) | Low | Medium |\n| **Code** | ~50 LOC | ~150 LOC | ~100 LOC | ~200 LOC + reimpl logic |\n| **Testing** | Easy (mock bridge) | Easy (mock HTTP) | Medium (mock spawn) | Hard (mock DB) |\n| **Monitoring** | Built-in (MCP) | Easy (HTTP metrics) | Hard (process) | Hard |\n| **Debugging** | Easy (logs) | Easy (network tools) | Medium | Hard |\n\n---\n\n## Final Verdict\n\n### **Primary: MCP Call (90% confidence)**\n\n**Use when:** neuroMemoryBridge exists or can be added with minimal effort\n\n**Why:**\n- Lowest latency for real-time queries\n- Leverages existing OpenClaw infrastructure\n- Type-safe contracts\n- Easy error handling\n- Preserves brain mechanism intelligence\n\n**Risk:** If MCP bridge doesn't exist, building it adds complexity. In that case, go REST.\n\n---\n\n### **Backup: REST API (75% confidence)**\n\n**Use when:** Building from scratch without MCP infrastructure\n\n**Implementation:**\n```python\n# neuro-memory/api.py\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.post(\"/api/v1/query/evolution\")\nasync def query_for_evolution(query: EvolutionQuery) -> EvolutionQueryResponse:\n    # Use brain mechanisms to process query\n    patterns = await brain.query_patterns(\n        query_type=query.type,\n        time_range=query.timeRange,\n        limit=query.limit\n    )\n    \n    return EvolutionQueryResponse(\n        success=True,\n        patterns=patterns,\n        metadata={\n            \"queryTime\": elapsed_ms,\n            \"brainMechanismsUsed\": [\"pattern_recognition\", \"temporal_analysis\"],\n            \"confidence\": 0.85\n        }\n    )\n```\n\n```typescript\n// evolution/neuroMemoryClient.ts\nexport async function queryNeuroMemory(query: EvolutionQuery): Promise<FailurePattern[]> {\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch('http://localhost:8001/api/v1/query/evolution', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify(query),\n      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(2000) // 2s timeout\n    });\n    \n    const data = await response.json();\n    return data.patterns || [];\n  } catch (error) {\n    logger.warn('Neuro-memory query failed, proceeding without', { error });\n    return []; // Evolution continues\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Go with MCP if you have it.** It's the right architecture for OpenClaw's ecosystem.\n\n**Go with REST if you don't.** It's standard, debuggable, and fast enough.\n\n**Avoid spawn and SQLite.** Spawn is too slow; SQLite loses the brain's intelligence.\n\n**Key principle:** Evolution should be *intelligent* about failures, not just aware of them. Neuro-memory's 8 brain mechanisms provide that intelligence—the query interface must preserve it."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"zhipu","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":3006,"output":3484,"cacheRead":11200,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":17690,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1773008075408}}
